# 11. An important historical distinction

These languages represent **different dimensions of programming evolution**.

Your previous computer-era model can actually connect to this nicely:

```text
Mainframe era
    │
    ├── FORTRAN
    ├── COBOL
    └── ALGOL
          │
          ↓
Minicomputer / UNIX era
    │
    └── C
          │
          ↓
PC era
    │
    ├── BASIC
    ├── Pascal
    ├── C
    └── C++
          │
          ↓
GUI / Windows era
    │
    ├── Visual Basic
    ├── Delphi
    └── C++
          │
          ↓
Internet era
    │
    ├── Java
    ├── JavaScript
    └── C++
```

The interesting thing is that **C, BASIC and Pascal represent three different answers to the same question:**

> How should humans program computers?

### C

> Give the programmer powerful control over the machine.

### BASIC

> Make programming accessible to ordinary people and beginners.

### Pascal

> Make programming structured, disciplined and teachable.

Then their descendants evolved:

```text
C
 ↓
C++
 ↓
powerful abstraction
```

```text
BASIC
 ↓
Visual Basic
 ↓
GUI + event-driven + RAD
```

```text
Pascal
 ↓
Object Pascal
 ↓
Delphi
 ↓
OOP + GUI + RAD
```

