## 12. One more connection to your programming-history model

Given your interest in the **"eras of programming languages"**, I would place these languages roughly like this:

```text
1950s
  │
  ├── FORTRAN
  ├── COBOL
  └── ALGOL
        │
        ↓
1960s–70s
  │
  ├── BASIC
  ├── Pascal
  └── C
        │
        ↓
1980s
  │
  ├── C++
  ├── Turbo Pascal
  └── BASIC dialects
        │
        ↓
1990s
  │
  ├── Visual Basic
  ├── Delphi / Object Pascal
  └── C++
        │
        ↓
2000s+
  │
  ├── C++
  ├── C#
  ├── Java
  └── many other descendants
```

And there is an especially interesting pattern:

**C and Pascal moved toward OOP**, while **BASIC moved toward visual/event-driven programming**.

So:

```text
             Structured Programming
              /        |        \
             /         |         \
            C       Pascal      BASIC
            |          |          |
            ↓          ↓          ↓
           C++    Object Pascal   VB
            |          |          |
            └──────┬───┴──────────┘
                   ↓
          Modern application
             programming
```

This is one reason learning the history of **C, C++, BASIC, Visual Basic, Pascal, and Object Pascal together** is valuable: they show three major branches of the transition from **early procedural programming → structured programming → object-oriented programming → GUI/event-driven programming**.
